1 NAME Marx (Mordechai) /Model/
2 GIVN Marx (Mordechai)
2 _MARNM Model
1 SEX M
1 OCCU Court Jew MARX MODEL (1686-1710 in Wallerstein taxlist) printer in Ansbach - moved to Pfalz-Neuburg after 1716 ?
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The court factor Marx Model was the Frankish pendant to the court Jews Joseph Süss Oppenheimer in Stuttgart, Leffmann Behrens in Hannover or Mayer Amschel Rothschild in Kassel. Model was the first court Jew in the 
2 CONC Margravate of Brandenburg-Ansbach. Since the 1680s he started to play a dominant economic role in the principality. At the end of the century Model owned the best funded businesses in the Margravate and started to operate beyond t
2 CONC he Frankish borders. Around 1708 he got into business with the Vienna Court Chamber and concluded a contract about the marketing of salt from Donauwörth in Wuerttemberg. Model’s large fortune also made him the largest money lender
2 CONC  in Brandenburg-Ansbach. The Margrave gave him special privileges - e.g. only Model, who had set up a printing press in Fürth in 1691, was allowed to print the Talmud in the Margravate – and supported him to collect debts due to h
2 CONC im. 7
2 CONT At the beginning of the 18th century Model was however supplanted by intrigues of his archrival Elkan Fränkel (1675-1720) from Fürth. Fränkel was able to keep the confidence of the Margrave for several years until he in turn becam
2 CONC e the victim of intrigues by the Model family. In 1712 he was imprisoned in Würzburg, where he died in 1720. Abraham Elias Model, the grandson of Marx Model, continued the family tradition then, he became "Churpfälzischer and Ansb
2 CONC acher Cabinets Factor" and "Cabinet und Cammer Factor" of Oettingen-Wallerstein. He amassed a huge fortune through salt trade and financial transactions and was able to grant a large credit to the Elector Karl Philipp. The present
2 CONC  City Hall in Monheim, which was the residence of Abraham Elias until the expulsion of the Jews from Monheim, is an impressive testimony of the former prosperity of the Model family. Marx Model was at the peak of his political and
2 CONC  economic power at the end of the 17th century. He was not only the best-funded entrepreneur, but also the largest lender. Not only the Margrave himself, but also numerous Frankish and Swabian noble family took out loans from him.
2 CONC  Model’s debtors include among others the Barons von Stein and the Barons von Crailsheim, and, as we learn from the present document, the Counts of Castell.
