2 CONT (Replacement only of  3/24/2008 profile)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT In the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries, many well-known rabbis held positions in the   Pinczôw (Coordinates:  50°32′N;  20°32′E ) in Świętokrzyskie Voivodship, about 40 km south of Kielce.  It is the capital of Pińczów County
2 CONC .   community. These rabbis included:
2 CONT
2 CONT Rav Shmuel Tzvi Hersh (1615-1635);
2 CONT
2 CONT  Rav Yitzchak Katz (died 1655);
2 CONT
2 CONT  Rav Yitzhak Yechihel Michael Shapira (died 1644);
2 CONT
2 CONT  Rav Naftali Hirsh ben Rav Binyamin Wolf (1675);
2 CONT
2 CONT  Rav Moshe Yitzhak Yehuda Leib Tsonz (died 1684);
2 CONT
2 CONT  Rav Shaul Katzenelbogen;
2 CONT
2 CONT Rav Asher Anzil ben Rav David (died 1716);
2 CONT
2 CONT Rav Yosef ben Rav Yehuda Leib;
2 CONT
2 CONT Rav Shimon Wolf ben Rav Yaakov Yankel, author of "Kavod HaBayit" (1740);
2 CONT
2 CONT Rav Yitzhak Avraham ben Rav Dov Berish Katz (1743-1773);
2 CONT
2 CONT Rav Shlomo, author of "Vayakhail Shlomo" (the grandfather of Rav Shlomo of Radomsk);
2 CONT
2 CONT Rav Avraham Gavriel (1783);
2 CONT
2 CONT Rav Avraham Katz Rapaport (died 1809), author of "Keter Kahunah";
2 CONT
2 CONT and Rav Aryeh Lieb.
2 CONT
2 CONT Subsequently, all the Rabbis of the community came from the Rapaport family — Rav Dov Barish (died 1832); his son Rav Israel HaKohen (died 1853); his son Rav Shaul (died 1895); and his son, Rav Shabatai Rapaport, the last Rabbi of
2 CONC  Pinczôw. With the spread of Hassidism
2 CONT
2 CONT "Kielce-Radom SIG Journal Volume 5, Number 1 Winter 2001" ________________________________________________________
