1 OCCU Silk merchant and Intellectual
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Gelber’s History of Brody 
2 CONT
2 CONT p.181-2
2 CONT
2 CONT Yaacob Shmuel Bik (1770-1831) was one of founders of the Galician Haskala. He came from a well known family in Brody. He was, apparently, the grandson of Shmuel Bik, son in law of Rabbi R Arie Lib Teumin. He wasn’t an enthusiast o
2 CONC f the great German poets, as most of the Maskilim of his generation. He admired English and French literature, and he was the only one to translate English and French poems into Hebrew. 
2 CONT   As a member  of the Bik family, who kept trade relations with western Europe, he lived the great traders's life, enjoyed European culture and knew French which he learned from his friend Moshe Kohn. Bik had strong relations with
2 CONC  Lviv's maskilim [names=] and often went to Lviv for meetings there.  With the arrival of Lepin to Brody for the second time (1808-1817), his life changed.  He was his pupil in 1813 when Lepin was attacked for his Yiddish translat
2 CONC ion of Míshlê  - Bik, in a letter to Tovia Pheder, defended his teacher and express his opinion regarding the Yiddish as the Jewish people's language. [p. 183-185] in 1817 Bik published a hand-written Hebrew journal "O'lat Shabbat
2 CONC '  in Brody. Nothing remains of that journal.  In 1823 Bik took part in the collection "Ha'tsfira" (תקפ"ד)  edited by M. Letris, RN"K (Nachman Kohen Krochmal) , Shalom Ha'cohen, David Friedlander, Avraham Goldberg and Yehuda Lib M
2 CONC ises, with the essay "to the Maskilim of our people." ... With great love of his people he writes that the cultural and economical state of the Jewish people is bound first to the general policy and culture of the state. In times 
2 CONC of liberty – the Jews would have it good – in bad times – the persecutions would emerge. The Emancipation starting with the French revolution brought about an easement for the Jews...
2 CONT
2 CONT [p. 190] Shmuel Yaacob Bik gathered around him several pupils, most of them among the sons of the trader clerks who studied at the Real Schule established in Brody in 1815. His daughter Hinda, married his pupil Isaac Ozer Rotenber
2 CONC g. His other only son Israel, who was very talented, died in the age of 22 during the Cholera Epidemic in Brody תק"צ
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://www.yivoencyclopedia.org/article.aspx/Bick_Yaakov_Shemuel
2 CONT
2 CONT (1772–1831), Hebrew writer, satirist, translator, and merchant. Ya‘akov Shemu’el Bick was one of the most prominent figures in the first generation of Galician maskilim but was eventually drawn to Hasidism. He was born in Brody in
2 CONC  eastern Galicia, a major commercial town that became famous, in those years, as a focal point in the conflict between Hasidism and its opponents.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Bick family, one of the most respected and privileged in Galicia, had extensive trade connections throughout Europe, and young Ya‘akov Shemu’el soon was involved in the family business. At the same time, he studied languages a
2 CONC nd acquired extensive general knowledge in addition to his traditional Jewish education. Bick, who lived in Brody for most of his life, was closely associated with prominent Galician maskilim, including Naḥman Krochmal, Shelomoh Y
2 CONC ehudah Rapoport, and Dov Ber Gintsburg, and was regarded as a prominent student of Menaḥem Mendel Lefin of Satanów, who lived in Brody for a few years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1817, Bick published a Hebrew periodical titled ‘Olat Shabat, which was distributed in handwritten form and included contributions from prominent maskilim of his generation. He also contributed to the single issue of Me’ir Lett
2 CONC eris’s periodical Ha-Tsefirah, writing a historiosophic article “El maskile bene ‘amenu” (To the Enlightened among Our People; 1823), on the place of Jewish society among the nations. Bick advocated, among other things, the promot
2 CONC ion of wisdom, learning, and education in Galicia and stressed the importance of labor and agriculture.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bick translated English and French poetry into Hebrew, and these translations were published in Bikure ha-‘itim. He wrote several biographies, notably those of Menaḥem Mendel Lefin, Efrayim Zalman Margoliot (the chief rabbi of Bro
2 CONC dy), Yehudah Leib ben Ze’ev (of the Berlin maskilim), and others, references to which were found in his draft notebook. Additionally, he wrote satirical skits about Hasidism and the Hasidic world. The sparseness of his surviving w
2 CONC ritings is in no way indicative of a small body of work, however. Bick was a pillar of the first generation of Galician maskilim; he managed to assemble around him a large circle of students, members of the wealthiest Jewish famil
2 CONC ies of Brody and graduates of the Brody Natural Sciences Secondary School. Unfortunately, the fire that consumed large parts of Brody in 1835 also destroyed the bulk of Bick’s manuscripts. Some letters that survived the fire were 
2 CONC later published in Kerem ḥemed. Among other material that did not perish were his draft notebook and a few manuscripts that are available at Yosef Perl’s archive, such as Bick’s satire “Ḥezyone hitul” (Jest Visions), which was pub
2 CONC lished in 1954 by Dov Sadan.
2 CONT
2 CONT The issue of Bick’s ambivalent feelings toward Hasidism, which ultimately led him to abandon the Haskalah camp, is one of the most interesting stories in the history of early Haskalah in Eastern Europe. Originally Bick, as was the
2 CONC  case with most of his fellow maskilim, regarded Hasidism as a pernicious influence in Jewish society. This attitude was reflected in his outspokenly anti-Hasidic notes and satires (including the above-mentioned “Ḥezyone hitul” in
2 CONC  the early 1820s). At the same time, he was deeply interested in Hasidism and eager to acquire its literature.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1815, Bick was involved in a debate regarding Lefin’s controversial translation of the Book of Proverbs into Yiddish. (Lefin had translated parts of the Bible in order to make it more accessible to the masses.) Bick defended hi
2 CONC s mentor, formulating a principled stand that was sympathetic to Yiddish as a language and symbol for the masses and defying the dominant maskilic view that emphasized the exclusive use of Hebrew. This debate marked the beginning 
2 CONC of a gradual change in Bick’s views on Hasidism and the widening of his ideological rifts with the Haskalah. Bick accused the maskilim of proclaiming tolerance while fueling divisiveness and agitation within Jewish society, and he
2 CONC  demanded that they include Hasidism in their implementation of the concept of tolerance. Bick regarded the Hasidim as an integral part of the Jewish people and opposed the coercive measures initiated by such maskilim as Yosef Per
2 CONC l and Yehudah Leib Mieses, who approached the authorities to complain against Hasidim.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the mid-1820s, Bick’s ostentatious displays of sympathy to Hasidism and the dispute between him and his fellow maskilim reached a peak, and he abandoned the Haskalah camp, leaving behind confusion, discontent, and resentment. A
2 CONC t the same time, it is not absolutely clear whether Bick actually became a Hasid, and there is no direct evidence regarding his actual connections with the world of Hasidism.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bick devoted the last years of his life to public and community work in his hometown, Brody, becoming a prominent public figure and a respected community leader. He worked tirelessly and faithfully on behalf of his community durin
2 CONC g the days of the severe cholera epidemic that devastated Brody in 1831—an epidemic that eventually claimed his own life as well. Suggested Reading Ḥayim Shalom Ha-Levi, “Ya‘akov Shemu’el Bik,” Sinai 63 (1968): 252–279; Ephraim Ku
2 CONC pfer, “Ya‘akov Shemu’el Bik le-or te‘udot ḥadashot,” Gal-Ed 4–5 (1978): 535–547; Samuel Werses, “Yankev Shmuel Bik, der blondzhendiker maskil,” YIVO-bleter 13.7–8 (November–December 1938): 505–536; Samuel Werses, “Ha-Nusaḥ ha-meko
2 CONC ri ha-bilti yadu‘a shel igeret Shemu’el Ya‘akov Bik el Tuvyah Feder,” Kiryat sefer 58.1 (1983): 170–187; Samuel Werses, “Ben shene ‘olamot: Ya‘akov Shemu’el Bik ben ha-haskalah la-ḥasidut—Le-‘iyun me-ḥadash,” Gal-Ed 9 (1986): 27–7
2 CONC 6.
2 CONT
2 CONT BICK, JACOB SAMUEL:
2 CONT
2 CONT Jewish Encyclopedia.com
2 CONT
2 CONT  Isidore Singer, Peter Wiernik Austrian author; born in the eighteenth century; died in Brody, 1831. He was a satirical writer of force and ability, and one of the ablest pioneers of the "haskalah" (culture) movement among the Jew
2 CONC s of Galicia. His contributions to the "Bikkure ha-'Ittim," "Kerem Ḥemed," and other Hebrew publications of his time contain strong pleas for the spread of secular knowledge and industry among the Galician Hebrews; and, like all h
2 CONC is contemporaries among the Maskilim or progressionists, he was strongly in favor of agricultural pursuits by Jews. He died of cholera in 1831 and left several manuscript works, both in prose and poetry, which were burned in the g
2 CONC reat conflagration in Brody in the spring of 1835, when the house of his son-in-law, Isaac Rothenberg, was totally destroyed. Bick was highly respected for his piety, learning, and ability; and the destruction of his literary rema
2 CONC ins was at the time deplored as a great loss. Bibliography: 	•	Kerem Ḥemed, i., Vienna, 1833, note to Letter 22; 	•	ib. ii. 131.
