2 CONT GorliceFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopediaJump to: navigation, search Gorlice 
2 CONT
2 CONT Coordinates: 49°40′N 21°10′E
2 CONT
2 CONT Town square 
2 CONT
2 CONT Flag  Coat of arms 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gorlice  Coordinates: 49°39′N 21°10′E﻿ / ﻿49.65°N 21.167°E﻿ / 49.65; 21.167 
2 CONT
2 CONT Country  Poland  Voivodeship Lesser Poland  County Gorlice County 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gmina Gorlice (urban gmina) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Government 
2 CONT  • Mayor Witold Kochan  Area 
2 CONT  • Total 23.56 km2 (9.10 sq mi)  Population (2008) 
2 CONT  • Total 29,500 
2 CONT  • Density 1,300/km2 (3,200/sq mi)  Time zone CET (UTC+1) 
2 CONT  • Summer (DST) CEST (UTC+2)  Postal code 38-300 and 38-320  Car plates KGR  Website http://www.gorlice.pl 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gorlice (pronounced [ɡɔrˈlʲit​͡sɛ], Ukrainian: Горлиці, Horlytsi; Yiddish: גאָרליץ-Gurlitz) is a city and an urban municipality ("gmina") in south eastern Poland with around 29,500 inhabitants (2008). It is situated south east of 
2 CONC Kraków and south of Tarnów between Jasło and Nowy Sącz in the Lesser Poland Voivodeship (since 1999), previously in Nowy Sącz Voivodeship (1975–1998). It is the capital of Gorlice County.
2 CONT
2 CONT The city lies in the Ropa and Sękówka River Valleys, surrounded by several mountain ranges of the Carpathian Mountains, namely their part called Beskid Niski (Low Beskids) massive. It is located in the heartland of the Doły (Pits)
2 CONC , and its average elevation above sea level is 380 metres (1,247 ft), although there are some more considerable hills located within the confines of the city. The city is nowadays situated in a heavily populated region 14.6 miles 
2 CONC from Jasło, 21.2 miles from Nowy Sącz, 25.5 miles from Tarnów, and 62.6 miles from Kraków.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gorlice was founded during the reign of Casimir the Great in 1354 by German settlers from Görlitz (see also Walddeutsche). The city was the focal point of the German Gorlice-Tarnów Offensive during World War I, in May, 1915. Hence
2 CONC  the "Gorlice fair" or "Gorlice days" held every year during the May Bank Holidays and adjoining days, which are enjoyed by many visitors both domestic and from abroad.
2 CONT
