1 NAME Alois Peter Ignatz /Porges/
2 GIVN Alois Peter Ignatz
2 SURN Porges
2 _MARNM Porges Edler von Portheim
2 NICK Ignatz Porges
1 NAME Alois Peter Ignatz Porges Edler von Portheim
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1 AUG 1815
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Kaiserreich Österreich-Ungarn
1 DEAT
2 DATE 18 FEB 1900
2 ADDR
3 CITY Breslau
3 STAE Preussen
3 CTRY Deutschland
1 OCCU Merchant, Partner in Hosiery Factory Portheim & Hinkel, Chemnitz, Saxony
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Birth Familiant http://www.badatelna.eu/fond/2098/reprodukce/?zaznamId=401695&reproId=590912
2 CONT
2 CONT Born Ignatz Porges, he changed his name to Alois Peter von Portheim when he and his brothers were elevated to the aristocracy with the title "Edler", for their contribution to industry. Their porcelain factory was famous for the p
2 CONC ink-coloured clay used. A precondition to becoming an aristocrat in the Austrian Empire was being pf the Catholic religion. This meant Alois had to change his faith. His wife, Helene Hinkel, was the sister of a hosiery manufacture
2 CONC r in Chemnitz, Friedrich Otto Hinkel, who was also a town councillor there. (The family's religion was evangelical Lutheran, it is not known whether she also became a Catholic). They were married in 1842. Alois was in business wit
2 CONC h Otto in 1855, as is shown by the entry in the Chemnitz trade directory:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1855  Johannisgraben 19, Chemnitz Geschäft Portheim und Hinkel, Klostergraben 19 in Chemnitz In the register of inhabitants: Kaufmann; Strumpfwaaren-Manufactur, Firma: Portheim und Hinkel, (Klostergraben 19)
2 CONT
2 CONT As Helene died in 1890, there is nobody alive today who remembers why Alois died in Breslau. He was 84 years old in 1900 and is unlikely to have been on a business trip. Perhaps one of his children was living there. A search for h
2 CONC is death record in Breslau might provide more information.
