1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Reference: [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-1-26035021-1-154/babette-brucha-loeb-born-breisach-in-myheritage-family-trees?indId=externalindividual-5330b0517b10bdf7fb63314122b75cee&mrid=d31c29bb44b163b1
2 CONC 781721cac37a41f0 MyHeritage Family Trees] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Feb 28 2019, 6:41:58 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT Babette LOEB nee BREISACH, death record. Date of death: 5 Aug 1861 Age: 92 (born circa 1768). Birthplace: Pressburg. Place of death: Leopoldstadt 378 by weakness, merchant's widow, buried at Waehring Jewish cemetery, group 8, row 
2 CONC 4, lot 20 (2620/8-127). (from H. Weiss, IKG Vienna)
2 CONT
2 CONT Brucha Breisach Entry. Pinkas of the Chevra Kaddisha [Burial Society] of Mattersdorf, Hungary, (1936-1810) p. 94b , Microfilm Reel 8180, Central Archives for the History of the Jewish People Jerusalem, JXIV-1/1. On 2 Oct 1850, Bru
2 CONC cha Breisach the wife of Katzin Yehiel Segal made a testament where she established an endowment fund for the benefit of her soul and the soul of her husband, (may he live and be well to 120). Brucha was the daughter of David Brei
2 CONC sach of Pressburg, the wife Yehiel Segal from Mattersdorf. [Researched and translated by Yitzchok Stroh, 2006]
2 CONT
2 CONT Brucha Breisach the wife of Yehiel Segal endowment fund. Pinkas of the Chevra Kaddisha [Burial Society] of Mattersdorf, Hungary, p. 112, Microfilm Reel 8179 frame 137, Central Archives for the History of the Jewish People Jerusale
2 CONC m. The entry in the Keren Kayemes ledger noted that the Yartzheit of Brucha Breisach the wife of Yehiel Segal was on the 2nd day of the new moon of Elul. Her mother's name was Frumat. She gave her house to her sons Abraham Löb and
2 CONC  his brother Itzik Segal. Her grandsons (or whomever later owns the house) are obligated to make a contribution to the fund on her Yartzheit. The fund was still being maintained until 1884, the last person to pay was Shmuel --?-- 
2 CONC [Researched and translated by Yitzchok Stroh]
